                <h2>Weekly Watch: AI Copy Homogenization Is Eating Into Your Ad Performance</h2>
<p>This week, we noticed a significant drop in CTR across multiple overseas ad accounts. After investigation, the problem wasn't targeting or bidding — it was that AI-generated ad copy was becoming increasingly "cookie-cutter."</p>
<p>When teams started using ChatGPT, Claude, and similar tools to write ad copy at scale, efficiency improved, but so did the homogenization problem. When everyone's AI outputs similar content, users experience fatigue, platform competition intensifies, and CPC quietly climbs.</p>
<p>This isn't an isolated case — it's an industry trend.</p>
<h2>Root Cause: Why Does AI Copy Tend to "Collide"?</h2>
<p>AI tools generate content based on existing data. When numerous ad teams use similar prompts to ask AI for "compelling ad copy," the outputs naturally converge.</p>
<p>AI copy homogenization typically stems from three sources:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Template-style prompts</strong>: Team AI instructions lack differentiation, relying on fixed structures long-term</li>
<li><strong>Scarce copy library</strong>: No internal quality copy database, forcing AI to recombine limited information</li>
<li><strong>Insufficient human intervention</strong>: Publishing AI drafts directly without optimization based on account data</li>
</ul>
<h2>Direction 1: Make AI Speak "Human Language"</h2>
<p>The goal isn't making AI write more eloquently — it's making it write more specifically.</p>
<p>Try adding more detailed constraints to your prompts:</p>
<pre><code>Write 3 ad copies for our cookware DTC site, each targeting a specific use scenario (e.g., &quot;warming milk for kids late at night&quot;). Avoid vague phrases like &quot;high quality&quot; or &quot;excellent performance.&quot; Each copy must include at least one number or specific feature description.
</code></pre>
<p>This "scenario-based + specific" instruction approach effectively widens the variation in AI outputs.</p>
<h2>Direction 2: Build an Internal Copy素材库</h2>
<p>Start this simple task today: archive every high-performing copy.</p>
<p>Organize by these dimensions:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Category</th>
<th>Example</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Product Type</td>
<td>Home, 3C, Beauty, Apparel</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Copy Style</td>
<td>Story-driven, Feature-focused, Urgency, Emotional</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Conversion Goal</td>
<td>Add-to-cart, Purchase, Lead capture</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>When AI generates new copy, let it extract from your素材库 for rewriting rather than starting from scratch.</p>
<h2>Direction 3: Never Skip Human Secondary Processing</h2>
<p>AI is an efficiency tool, but it can't replace投放人员的 judgment.</p>
<p>Establish a simple SOP: <strong>AI Draft → Human Review → Data Validation</strong>. During review, focus on three points:</p>
<ol>
<li>Does the copy align with current market aesthetics in your target region?</li>
<li>Did you accidentally use competitor buzzwords (replace if so)?</li>
<li>Is there a clear, natural CTA?</li>
</ol>
<p>One simple human intervention can transform a mediocre AI draft into a high-converting asset.</p>
<h2>Action Items for This Week</h2>
<p>To address the copy homogenization issues exposed this week, execute these two items immediately:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Short-term (this week)</strong>: Spot-check all AI-generated copy from the past 30 days, identify and pause the bottom 20% performers, and replace with differentiated versions</li>
<li><strong>Mid-term (this month)</strong>: Build your team's proprietary copy素材库, establish AI prompt optimization guidelines, and incorporate human review into standard workflow</li>
</ul>
<p>AI is a powerful tool, but tools require proper use. When competitors mass-produce generic AI copy, your competitive edge lies in "thinking one step further, polishing one more detail."</p>
